WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.050
ED ANUFF: So I'm going
to talk to you today.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.025
So first let me
introduce myself.

00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:13.150
I'm Ed Anuff, head of product
strategy at Apigee at Google.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.040
And what I'm going
to talk to you today

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.200
about is this idea of
API first thinking,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:27.040
how you should think about your
APIs, those digital products,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.690
some of the different ways that
APIs get shared and reused.

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:37.120
And how you need to think about
APIs as part of an ecosystem

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.130
rather than just simply
as a way that you

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:47.770
are exposing your applications
as digital services.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.830
So, one of these--

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:54.130
then there's me, let
me skip past that--

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:58.520
generally when we talk
about API first thinking,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.450
what we mean by
that is this idea

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.730
that the starting
point of your APIs

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.330
shouldn't simply be what you
have in the database, what you

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:11.560
have in your application
server, or in an enterprise

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.460
context, what you have in
your enterprise service bus.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.950
But really starting
from this idea of,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.100
what are the experiences
that you're trying to power?

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.320
And what we see in
sort of traditional IT

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.580
and also I think from sort
of a first order thinking

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.500
that most developers who
approach this problem come at

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.230
is to say, I have this stack.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.380
I have this application
that I built.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.570
I've got perhaps a legacy system
that I want to make available.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:48.700
And so APIs are just simply a
way that I expose this service.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.080
And depending on how I'm going
about thinking about my API

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.280
program, perhaps maybe
I'm assembling an API team

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.290
or maybe this is more of an
informal micro service context

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.070
and I'm just sort of
sharing in an ad hoc way,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.210
these are the services
I have available.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.190
But generally, there's this
idea of what at Apigee we would

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.110
call this digital value chain.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.060
Which is that you're exposing
these services and a variety

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.380
of participants, developers
go and use these APIs

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:24.490
and build applications.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:29.320
And they ultimately may show up
powering a set of applications

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.030
that a user actually
interacts with.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:38.020
And API first thinking
basically reverses that.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.860
And it goes and says, OK,
there are a set of experiences

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.910
that I need to deliver.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:47.590
There's a set of partnerships
that I want to build.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:53.340
Perhaps I want to create
new types of business models

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.780
that are made possible by APIs.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.780
And I need to start
with that end in mind

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.530
and design my APIs
to meet those needs.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.080
I need to think about what
the developer experience is

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:05.762
going to be.

00:03:05.762 --> 00:03:07.720
I need to think about
what the terms of service

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.310
are going to be.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.370
And I'm going to craft an
API program around that.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:19.110
And most people that we see
start from that previous slide.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.230
But the really
successful API programs,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.450
the companies that you
see that are actually

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.452
based on APIs, so we all
see companies like Twilio

00:03:28.452 --> 00:03:30.660
and Stripe that have been
very successful in building

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.270
API based businesses,
they start in this way.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.880
They start thinking about
the developer experience

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.050
and they start thinking
about as much as possible,

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:42.940
the user experiences that these
APIs may ultimately power.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.550
So, given that that's the
case, why don't we do?

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.350
This why doesn't
everybody do this?

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.930
Well, the answer is there's a
lot of different types of APIs.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.430
In fact, there are really three
different ways of viewing APIs.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.100
And one of the things
that I hope you take away

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.730
from this presentation is
whenever the API discussion

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.230
starts up in your
development teams

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.470
within your company, in
whatever context people start

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:16.657
talking about we should
pursue an API strategy,

00:04:16.657 --> 00:04:18.240
start to ask some
questions about what

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.560
do we mean by these APIs?

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.590
Because there's at least
three different forms of APIs.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.389
There's APIs really from
a service perspective,

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:27.930
whether we're talking
about new style

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.950
micro services or old style
service oriented architecture.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.920
There's APIs as interactions.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.050
And then there's
APIs as products.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.020
And we'll look at each one of
these in this presentation.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.320
And all of these APIs
exist within ecosystems.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.810
That is the fundamental
truth of an API

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.450
is that it is designed
to share services,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:54.300
share data, connect
experiences between systems,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.340
between applications,
between people.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.300
So you have to think about
the ecosystem consideration

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.710
regardless of what the purpose
is, which of these three types

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.600
of APIs that you're building.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.960
So, APIs as services,
if you're a developer,

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.180
if you're within IT or
whether you're in a startup,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.880
this is the area of
APIs that I think

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.470
most developers
sort of intuitively

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:23.880
understand because it's
what we live in, what we--

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.550
it's how we build our
software these days.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.940
But these APIs take a
lot of different forms,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.450
even within this context.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.180
The main way that
most developers end up

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.910
interacting with
APIs is in order

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.870
to get to the back end resources
that their applications need.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.870
And what we see is that if
you're building an application,

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.400
you need access
to data, you need

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.750
to be able to interact with the
outside world perhaps via email

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:52.530
services.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.320
Most people are not going and
using their company wide email

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:01.710
server to send out their
email interactions.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.170
They may be connecting
to an enterprise service

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.410
bus or some form of
integration architecture

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.740
if they're part
of an enterprise.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.781
Those of you in the
platform as a service space,

00:06:12.781 --> 00:06:15.030
if you're using Cloud Foundry,
if you're using Heroku,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.550
this idea of the
12 factor model is

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.490
going to be very familiar
to you and this idea

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.250
that your application
workloads can

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.450
scale better if you separate
out the stateful services

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.560
and treat them as
external services.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.080
So this might be a
way that you come

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.430
to start thinking about APIs
because this is how you're

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.047
going to get your
data or how you're

00:06:37.047 --> 00:06:38.880
going to get it your
storage or perhaps even

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.700
to an external service.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.000
And obviously, there's a
whole set of companies,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.670
Google included-- this is the
nature of the Google Cloud

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:51.750
Platform-- that are providing
you with a set of capabilities

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.390
that are going to be used
to power your applications.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.400
Interestingly enough, in
the enterprise space--

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.566
and some of you are in
enterprise space, some of you

00:06:58.566 --> 00:07:01.650
are in the startup space--
we see a big divide.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.000
In the enterprise space,
most of these services

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.560
that we talk about are probably
sitting behind your firewall,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.450
perhaps in your own data center.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.540
Whereas if you are doing
Greenfield new development

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.940
if you're a startup,
hopefully you

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.310
are getting these
from the variety

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:24.150
of awesome databases of service
or storage service choices

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:25.200
that you have.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.330
But even if you're
doing this internally,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:28.950
you're still thinking this way.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:34.620
We see very few companies
that are going and approaching

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.010
building new internal services
for their applications

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.380
where they're not now
thinking about saying,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.710
how do we make these
RESTful services, how

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:44.340
do we make these API based?

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.560
And in fact, increasingly going
and using the popular developer

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:55.350
portal type of format of
allowing internal developers

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.049
to discover and use these APIs.

00:07:57.049 --> 00:07:58.590
And there's a lot
of interesting work

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.790
that's being done here to
replace this ESV approach.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.600
In the Cloud Foundry world
and the Kubernetes world,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.860
there's this concept
of a service broker

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.950
that provides a better
level of mechanism

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.760
for integrating these services.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.750
Now inevitably in
these conversations,

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.860
we get questions
about micro services.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:25.360
And in fact, at Apigee as
we interact with customers,

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.200
this becomes the most
common topic these days.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.030
Which is wait a
minute, we're talking

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.260
about doing micro services.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:36.400
What's the difference between
APIs and micro services?

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.980
And also by extension,
what's the difference

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.470
between micro services
service oriented architecture?

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:44.550
So there's a lot of
definitions of this,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.130
of micro services
that float around.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.560
The one that I would
have really stuck

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:53.680
to when people would ask
me what the difference

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.226
between micro
services and so is,

00:08:55.226 --> 00:08:57.850
is to really talk about the fact
that micro services are trying

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:00.250
to solve a scalability problem.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:03.160
They're trying to solve the
challenge of decomposing

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.670
your application into
independent units of work that

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.490
can be scaled at appropriate
levels, the right container

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:11.980
instances for those workloads.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.260
Each one can be elastically
scaled independently.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:19.810
Micro services
generally are not meant

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:24.820
to be a mechanism of reuse.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.670
Again, the best analogy
to micro services

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:30.010
are, we took the components
of the spring world

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.500
and now we're laying
these out into containers.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.860
It lets us do things like
have polyglot programming

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:39.070
within our development teams.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.630
These are all good reasons
to use micro services.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.880
Not to say that you can't do
micro services for the purpose

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.820
of sharing and
reuse, but you need

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:53.860
to understand you are going
to pay a penalty in terms

00:09:53.860 --> 00:10:00.170
of agility the wider you cast a
net of your service dependency

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:00.670
graph.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.500
The more people who
depend on your services,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.590
the more you will
translate from being--

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:08.186
or transform from being a very
agile application development

00:10:08.186 --> 00:10:10.060
team, which may have
been the way you started

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.490
this journey, to being
a service provider who

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:17.480
now has to worry about a whole
bunch of other considerations.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.730
And it becomes a full
time job in and of itself.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.370
These are the ecosystem
considerations

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.020
I was referring to
previously, which

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.894
I'm going to get into
a little bit more depth

00:10:24.894 --> 00:10:27.130
later in this presentation.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.640
So the next form of APIs,
and this really throws

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.310
a lot of people for
a loop, is this idea

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:35.295
of APIs as interactions.

00:10:35.295 --> 00:10:40.150
And in fact, as we've
worked with enterprises,

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:43.540
and most enterprises really had
embraced this idea of service

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:47.130
oriented architecture
in the early 2000s,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.600
and there were a
set of blueprints

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.700
and patterns for reuse
that designing services--

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.160
the level of service
granularity and so forth--

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.600
really were encouraged
as best practices.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.390
Those proved to be
anti-patterns when you

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:06.520
went to move towards mobile.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.920
And they actually further
hampered a lot of people

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:11.347
as they tried to
embrace things like IoT.

00:11:11.347 --> 00:11:13.180
And there were a number
of reasons for this.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:16.480
But the idea was
that these services

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.390
that you use to power
front end interactions

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.400
look very different
than services

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.180
that you use to expose
back end resources.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:27.910
They are purpose built.
These experienced APIs--

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:31.690
that's a term that Netflix
coined to describe these

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:35.080
and it's caught on for people
who sort of study different API

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.670
design patterns--

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:39.580
look very different
than the services

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.040
that are designed for wide
reuse that perhaps you're

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.140
using when you're
building a back end

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:47.690
service that you want to be used
by many different applications.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.500
And in fact, the lifespan
of these is very different.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.230
In the mobile world, you further
compound this with the fact

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:54.880
that many mobile
developers, if there's

00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:00.250
no SDK to access these APIs,
consider the API incomplete.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.350
And so that's why
when you see companies

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:05.410
that are trying to succeed
with mobile developers,

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:06.320
if you look at--

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.100
for example, Firebase
provides a real first class

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.240
mobile experience.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:18.250
They do so by providing very
well-built SDKs that developers

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:23.020
can easily integrate into
whatever IDE environment

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.560
that they're using,
Xcode, what have you.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.800
They've put a lot of thought
into this developer experience.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:33.400
Most API developers, most API
architects that I talk to,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:39.160
they sort of want to
push as much as possible,

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.160
this SDK problem
off to the side.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:43.690
You do see some good approaches.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:47.050
A lot of the popularity
of Swagger now, open API,

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:50.800
has come about because it allows
you to express these APIs a way

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:56.470
that can quickly be accessed
via SDKs, client libraries that

00:12:56.470 --> 00:13:00.070
can be auto generated
from the APIs.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.230
Now experience APIs
add a new wrinkle

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:05.800
in that some of these
again are purpose built.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.450
They are not designed for reuse.

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:09.954
They may be built by your
front end developers.

00:13:09.954 --> 00:13:11.620
So you may have one
team that's building

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.810
your APIs for general
reuse but you've

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:18.070
got your web developers who are
building single page apps who

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.910
need special APIs to
power those experiences.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.740
And you may have the
aforementioned mobile clients

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:30.460
that again, needs specific APIs
to power specific experiences.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:33.760
Anybody who goes to a shopping
site, you go to Amazon,

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:35.440
you see a Buy It Now button.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:37.900
Obviously downstream,
you are going

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:44.170
to go and have an inner API that
handles the payment processing

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:47.290
but there's going to be
a separate interaction

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:50.001
or experience API that's
powering that Buy It Now

00:13:50.001 --> 00:13:50.500
button.

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:52.490
This is not an anti-pattern.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:54.850
You can have multiple
forms of APIs

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:56.770
to power multiple
forms of interactions.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:14:00.160
The way you do that is by
having this mediation layer.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.550
And so, companies that are
building user experiences

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:08.080
around APIs have some
form of mediation layer.

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:11.290
That might be something
that was built in Node.js.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:15.610
In fact, a lot of the popularity
for Node has come from the fact

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:19.990
that because it's JavaScript,
because JSON is the native data

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:24.580
format, it's a great
place to go and basically

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:29.575
create this mediation tier that
can go and take this back end

00:14:29.575 --> 00:14:31.950
inner API that might've been
designed for general purpose

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:35.110
use and create these small
services that are purpose built

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:38.350
for perhaps a specific
interaction when user hits

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:40.390
a button on your mobile app.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.070
The role of API
management platforms,

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.460
if you want to know why do
a lot of companies deploy

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.730
technologies like Apigee?

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:52.120
API management platforms provide
this mediation layer type

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:52.780
mechanism.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.180
And so, this might
be a reason why

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:59.230
you find yourself looking
at that sort of product

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.420
or technology.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:06.640
So the third aspect of
APIs is API as products.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:11.530
And this tends to
really separate out

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:15.610
the thinking from those
two previous instances.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.070
In the scenarios where
you're thinking about APIs

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:21.580
as services, it's
really your developers,

00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:24.820
your software
development team leads

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.700
that perhaps are thinking
about what services are

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:29.470
being designed for wider reuse.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:33.004
In APIs as interactions, it's
probably a front end team.

00:15:33.004 --> 00:15:35.170
But now you've got the
scenario where you go and say

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:37.840
I want to build a business
model around APIs.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.760
And in that case,
what you're doing is

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.790
you're really
thinking about how you

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:45.460
are going to manage
these APIs as products.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:49.780
And this tends to trip
people up because there's

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:55.262
a number of different folks
involved in building APIs.

00:15:55.262 --> 00:15:57.220
You've got your developers
that build the APIs.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.710
You have a set of people
who are testing them.

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.830
You have a whole set of
operational concerns,

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.080
whether you're doing sort
of classic enterprise style

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.320
centralized ops
or whether you're

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.260
using more of a DevOps
focus, you still

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:15.280
have a whole set of
considerations around that.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.230
Now you've got to
think about how

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.180
you manage this API lifecycle.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:20.800
And what we've seen
is that companies

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.300
that are successful
with this have

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.790
somebody in the role
of API product manager.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:27.940
And it's not always
a full time job.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:31.270
It might be part
of another role.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.880
But there is
somebody whose job it

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:38.350
is to make sure that all aspects
of the API lifecycle and most

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:40.660
importantly, the
critical metrics that

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:44.380
determine whether the
API is succeeding,

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:46.660
are being looked after.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.120
So when you're
productizing an API,

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:51.550
suddenly you now
have considerations

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:53.560
and if you're this
API product manager,

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.950
you're very focused
on your API design.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.020
If you're building a
payment processing API--

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:05.829
we've got a number of
customers that are offering

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:07.694
payment transaction gateways.

00:17:07.694 --> 00:17:09.819
If you're the product
manager of one of those APIs,

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:11.290
you're going and
saying, how can I

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:14.109
provide a better
developer experience

00:17:14.109 --> 00:17:16.900
than someone like Stripe?

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:20.500
And the way you're doing that is
through superior documentation,

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:24.760
perhaps better client
SDKs, and you're

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:27.220
looking at the design
of the API itself.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:30.800
Is the amount of parameters I
have to send or these payloads,

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:31.900
are they the right size?

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:34.233
Are they easy for developers
to wrap their heads around?

00:17:34.233 --> 00:17:35.620
Or do I have to
consider and send

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.930
a lot of data that
really doesn't make sense

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:38.980
for my application.

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:40.600
That's where API
design comes in.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:43.260
Is it properly designed
from RESTful best practices?

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:44.950
A lot of new security concerns.

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:47.420
One of the things
that we've seen

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.140
as we've been in a lot
of these API hackathons

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:54.460
is that the biggest
challenge people have,

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:58.450
developers in using a new API
is getting the authentication

00:17:58.450 --> 00:17:59.540
right.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:00.440
It's a big problem.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:01.815
Simply just going
and saying it's

00:18:01.815 --> 00:18:04.760
OAuth isn't always the solution.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:07.880
There's still a
lot of things that

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:11.390
need to be improved
in getting developers

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:13.844
to be able to understand
and be able to go and make

00:18:13.844 --> 00:18:16.010
a cURL call that allows
them to actually get results

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:17.360
from the API.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.490
And then the last piece,
and this is probably

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:20.960
the biggest set
of considerations

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:25.160
that the would be API product
manager needs to think about,

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:27.140
is the metrics for success.

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:29.320
This is the difference
between APIs as a product

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:33.450
and just APIs as a project.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.340
Your metrics for
success from an API

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:39.420
are going to be different,
depending on your business.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.120
John Musser, the founder
of ProgrammableWeb,

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:46.080
several years ago
made the observation

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.210
that I agree with,
that you need to--

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:51.450
regardless of the context
of what your APIs are for,

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:55.675
you need to go and think about
the classic marketing funnel.

00:18:55.675 --> 00:18:58.050
Some of you are going and
saying, hey, I'm building APIs.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:18:59.370
Does this mean I'm
a marketing person?

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:00.460
Well, not necessarily.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:02.700
But you have to think
about how people

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:05.259
are going to be adopting
these and every step

00:19:05.259 --> 00:19:06.300
that we're going through.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.990
And so he identified a set
of metrics and a process

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.480
that goes along with
those metrics from finding

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.720
developers, perhaps
promoting your API.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.270
It gets picked up
on Hacker News,

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:19.500
it gets found via
Google searches,

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:23.400
people are tweeting
about it, what have you.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:27.000
You're getting them to
go to a developer portal.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.470
They're signing up for it.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:30.240
They're using it
within an application.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:34.830
You've got active usage of that
API within a number of apps

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:36.540
that they shipped.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.900
Perhaps you've got monetization
associated with it.

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:42.380
This is going to be
different for you.

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:44.230
Everybody's API
business is different.

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:46.800
Some of this won't apply because
you're doing internal APIs.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:49.020
But even in internal
API ecosystems,

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.500
there will be a set of metrics
that you need to manage to.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.100
This is what it means to be a
service provider because you

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:57.760
need to know your customers.

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:00.400
And in order to provide
quality of service,

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:03.860
you need to be basically
managing to these metrics.

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:10.190
So, hopefully these three
forms of APIs make sense.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.770
Hopefully for those of you who
are involved in API projects,

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:15.080
you're able to think
about and map what you're

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:17.690
doing to these types of APIs.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.460
But what I want
to talk about now

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:23.660
is the ecosystem
that the APIs sit

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.790
within because this is where
things really get interesting.

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.930
It's not as simple as just
going and saying my exposing

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:34.040
my API is REST or I
see this GRPC thing.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:35.780
What are the technical
considerations?

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:38.990
Do I use a gateway
with caching or not?

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.090
All of those requirements
are very important

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:45.110
but it's the ecosystems
that your APIs are used at--

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:49.140
used by that really is the whole
reason why you're doing this.

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:51.810
Most businesses do
operate within ecosystems.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:55.530
If you are a company that is
becoming a digital business,

00:20:55.530 --> 00:21:01.080
you probably are already moving
many of your supply chains,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.050
your value chains to
be digitally mediated.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:08.250
And that might be what's
driving your API thinking.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:11.310
You may internally,
within your IT processes,

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:15.300
those represent a ecosystem
and a value chain.

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:18.930
Most of what we're talking about
here is what the people who get

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:21.590
all excited about this stuff--
tends to be industry analysts,

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:26.670
tends not so much to be
developers and IT people--

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.370
start talking about what
your value chains are.

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:33.460
Well, hopefully this will
serve as a primer to that.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:35.400
You generally, when
you're building APIs,

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.940
you're going to be serving
one or more ecosystems.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.870
You're going to have
your internal ecosystems,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.280
those are sort of the APIs
you share with your friends

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.890
on your team, your
internal developers,

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:47.844
but this ranges out.

00:21:47.844 --> 00:21:49.260
There might be
partner ecosystems,

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:50.718
there might be
industry ecosystems,

00:21:50.718 --> 00:21:52.260
there might be
public ecosystems.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.600
These all have new
considerations,

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:55.350
new requirements.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:22:00.180
Generally what we see
is people sort of take

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:03.180
this approach that I'm sorry
to say is a little naive

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:05.664
but that said, it's
the path everyone takes

00:22:05.664 --> 00:22:07.080
which is to go and
say, well we're

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.940
going to build our
APIs for internal usage

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:13.940
and then we're going to open
them up to the outside world

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:16.130
and it'll be great.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:20.520
Tends to-- you tend to find,
going back to my first slide,

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.380
that a whole bunch
of new considerations

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:23.296
hit you over the head.

00:22:23.296 --> 00:22:25.429
Hopefully halfway through
this presentation,

00:22:25.429 --> 00:22:27.720
you're starting to get an
inkling of what some of those

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:28.220
are.

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:33.990
But let's talk about how these
ecosystems are different.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.780
If you're doing ecosystems
for your APIs as services,

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:39.010
they're predominantly internal.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:43.815
These are what you're
doing to share and reuse

00:22:43.815 --> 00:22:44.730
the applications.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.063
You build the systems, you
build the back end resources,

00:22:47.063 --> 00:22:48.630
you build with other developers.

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:51.450
There's a number of
different approaches to this.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:53.910
There's the classic
SOA approach.

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:58.140
Now, people who were SOA
architects, SOA often--

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:01.740
SOA stands for service
oriented architecture.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.260
Those of us who were around
at the time go and say,

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.760
well there's nothing
wrong with SOA.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.490
It just simply says
we should build

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:09.990
as services that should
be designed for reuse

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:13.230
and you'll get all sorts of
productivity gains from that.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.170
What we found was that
most organizations ended up

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:20.190
finding that you ended
up having to impose

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.172
a strong form of
central governance

00:23:22.172 --> 00:23:23.380
in order to make this happen.

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:25.680
And there is a slippery
slope that gets you there.

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:28.740
And I actually believe that
about half the companies

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.240
that are embracing
micro services

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.040
are going to end up there.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:34.020
The way that you end up
on the slippery slope

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.180
is just going and
saying, all we have to do

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:39.600
is agree to a common
set of conventions

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:42.850
and all of our
services can be reused.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:46.320
Then nobody can agree with those
common set of conventions are.

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:49.920
Hence the strong central
governance model.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.410
Now, what tends to
work is this idea

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:54.840
of an API management model.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.550
An API model where
you go and say

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:02.410
look, I am going to expose
the API in this way.

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:06.610
It will be a RESTful API so all
of your clients can talk to it.

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.760
We are going to document
it in standard ways.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:13.540
We're going to use the open API
specification to document it.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:15.490
And I'm going to stand
up a developer portal

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:16.580
and that's where we're
going to find it.

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:18.880
And if that API doesn't
work the way you like,

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:25.350
then what you should do is
build your own client libraries

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:29.380
to access it and perhaps
use your own middleware,

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:29.890
if you will.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:32.470
Perhaps use Node.js
if it's something

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:35.050
you want to build yourself
to shape and convert

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:37.220
that API to your use cases.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:41.530
This tends to work very well.

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:47.650
We see that the interaction
model-- and that Node.js,

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:50.520
as I said before, that Node.js
model tends to be most closely

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:53.910
associated with situations where
these APIs need to be exposed

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.280
for interaction purposes--

00:24:56.280 --> 00:25:01.480
this has a lot of
interesting aspects to it.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.000
Mobile was the main
driver for this

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:06.430
but we now see things like
conversational computing.

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:10.000
We see things like if you're
using Google Assistant,

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:14.500
if you're using Alexa, if
you're using any of these, Siri.

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:19.150
You're seeing that you now have
the ability, if you actually

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:22.660
are delivering your services
within standard interaction

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:24.460
models, that they
can be now used

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:28.170
within these new types of
conversational interaction

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:28.670
models.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:30.086
But we also see
that there's a lot

00:25:30.086 --> 00:25:31.750
of business drivers for this.

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:34.150
Retail people look at this and--

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.130
folks in the retail
industry they go and say,

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:41.080
this is how I'm going to power
omnichannel interaction models.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:42.940
And you find that
there's this need

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:48.370
to go and make your APIs
available in standard ways

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:50.890
so that you can actually
talk to an e-commerce stack.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.800
You might be able to talk to
some form of direct response

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.860
email interaction
system and so on.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:58.344
These tend to try--

00:25:58.344 --> 00:26:00.010
so supporting an
ecosystem in this model

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:03.250
tends to try or drive
you to getting your APIs

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:04.780
to fit certain patterns.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:07.770
Right now, I say
patterns, not standards.

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:12.700
There aren't as many standards
in this area as there could be.

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:18.820
And I think that both
of these models, people

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:20.940
tend not to think of
themselves as participating

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:24.430
in an ecosystem but I think
increasingly you're going to.

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:26.640
Let me talk about the
product ecosystems

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:32.740
because I think those are
where things get the most

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:33.850
interesting.

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:36.830
So within the
product ecosystems,

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:39.010
there's a variety
of different ways

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:43.930
that people make their
APIs available for use

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:46.450
by other folks.

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:48.730
So basically, the idea
here is that you're

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:51.340
taking your business
capability, you're

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:53.890
packaging it up as
a building block

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:55.450
that other companies
can go and embed

00:26:55.450 --> 00:26:57.190
in their business processes.

00:26:57.190 --> 00:26:59.950
So, in terms of how
you find these APIs,

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:03.190
right now the predominant way
that people find and interact

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:06.580
with APIs is really sort of this
one sided traditional commerce

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:11.440
model where people go and
discover the API, usually

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:14.680
through a preexisting
business relationship,

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:20.230
or they are leveraging
one of the major brands.

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:24.790
If I'm going and I need to
send an SMS API, the API--

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:27.400
there's a set of choices,
very well-known choices.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:31.330
People learn about these
through conventional marketing

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:32.219
mechanisms.

00:27:32.219 --> 00:27:33.760
But where things
get more interesting

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:37.900
is where you have this
platform oriented approach

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:41.890
where you actually can bring
these APIs together and promote

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.060
them and combine them
in different ways.

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:46.210
Some of the examples
of this that we see

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:53.260
are for example, if you
look at the partnership

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:56.860
between MindBody which is--

00:27:56.860 --> 00:28:01.120
provides scheduling
services for things

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.530
like yoga studios, fitness
centers, and so on,

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:07.780
and what's happened
with Google Maps,

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:09.580
this is an example
of where you actually

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:13.210
see a two sided relationship
where both companies are

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:17.950
actually sharing services
and data between each other

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:20.350
to create two
different application

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:23.960
experiences with a whole
bunch of synergy between them.

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:27.130
So if I go to
Google Maps and I go

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:32.170
and I say I want to
be able to go and book

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:34.960
a session with a
fitness instructor,

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:36.730
I can go in and do that.

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:40.450
And Google Maps will call
out to the MindBody API

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:42.790
and they'll be able to
find the nearest fitness

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:46.810
center where that's available
and directly book it.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:50.020
That's what we actually
see in the right hand

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:54.700
side with this screen that's
showing Reserve with Google.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:59.650
I can get to this via
the map user experience.

00:28:59.650 --> 00:29:02.290
Now the flip side of
this is that you've

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:07.720
got Google Maps itself
integrating with MindBody

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:08.920
within its application.

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:13.150
So if MindBody is providing
a private label application

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:18.190
to a specific chain of fitness
centers or to end users

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:21.434
through the variety of
channels that they do that,

00:29:21.434 --> 00:29:24.040
that users are able
to go and get access

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:28.660
to Google Maps with all of
its location based services

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:32.500
but in the context of this
application experience.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:35.350
So I think that
what we're seeing

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:37.660
very successful
within the API spaces

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:40.510
where you're able to basically
cross-pollinate this experience

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:42.460
by sharing data and process.

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:44.050
Some of the other
forms of this slide

00:29:44.050 --> 00:29:45.550
are things that I
think you're going

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:49.990
to see more of as we look out
in the future of different forms

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:51.220
of ecosystems.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:53.470
One of the things I
wanted to leave you

00:29:53.470 --> 00:29:55.480
with at the end of
this presentation

00:29:55.480 --> 00:30:00.420
is this session that I just did
is the first part of what we're

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:06.210
calling The Connected Business
Track here at Google Next

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:11.550
to-morrow Chet Kapoor,
CEO of Apigee at Google,

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:14.100
is going to be talking
about this vision

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:18.030
of a connected business platform
and how all of these different

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:21.840
ecosystems power different
types of business models.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:25.260
And some of these business
models that we see

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:28.770
are actually these clearing
houses and marketplaces

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:32.100
for digital services and we'll
get into a lot more depth

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:34.650
in that keynote tomorrow.

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:38.930
So, let me wrap this up.

00:30:38.930 --> 00:30:41.540
So when we talk about
API first and we

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:45.500
talk about API centric thinking,
hopefully this presentation

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.260
has given you kind of this
perspective of thinking

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:50.510
about this outside in approach.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:53.300
Even if your APIs
are predominantly

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:57.770
being used for
internal purposes,

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:02.270
it makes sense if your scope
of reuse is wide enough.

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:04.984
If you are working with
other development teams that

00:31:04.984 --> 00:31:07.400
are sort of beyond arm's length
where you're not scrumming

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:09.441
with them, where you're
not regularly interacting

00:31:09.441 --> 00:31:11.780
with them on Slack,
you need to suddenly

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:16.340
be thinking about your API
consumers as customers.

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:22.684
Now if your APIs are designed
for being APIs as products,

00:31:22.684 --> 00:31:25.100
then you definitely are going
to have to think about this.

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:27.260
The competitiveness of
your API, the success

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:32.810
of your API as a product really
will come down to the developer

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:35.810
experience that you create
and the quality of service

00:31:35.810 --> 00:31:37.340
that you provide.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:41.390
Being able to identify what form
of API that you're building--

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:44.480
is it simply designed
for service reuse,

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:47.210
is it a micro service, is
it something that's powering

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:51.304
an interaction, or is it
this API as a product--

00:31:51.304 --> 00:31:52.970
is going to help you
think about how you

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:55.550
organize your team around it.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:57.725
Where it sits within
your organization.

00:31:57.725 --> 00:32:01.880
If it is designed
for API as a service

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:05.780
in that classical sense, maybe
it is something that sits--

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:07.460
if you're in an
IT organization--

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:11.540
that sits within your
classic integration

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:12.460
center of excellence.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:15.470
Although generally, we
see and advocate people

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:18.620
move away from that
strong centralized model.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.540
If it's an interaction,
it's probably

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:22.160
sitting with your
front end team.

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:25.330
But even if it is, you
now have considerations

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:27.950
like how do I allow
my front end team

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.690
to go and build these
interaction APIs

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:31.250
and make sure
they're still secure?

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:32.930
And if they're
APIs as a product,

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:35.240
then do you have an
API product manager?

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:39.670
This last concept of
managing your APIs

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:43.030
as a digital partnership,
super important.

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.490
This tends to be the light
bulb moment for most folks

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:49.635
when they think about
their API programs,

00:32:49.635 --> 00:32:51.010
particularly when
you're bridging

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:53.840
into trying to explain
the value of APIs

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:57.064
to the business folks
in your organizations.

00:32:57.064 --> 00:32:58.772
Suddenly when people
understand an API is

00:32:58.772 --> 00:33:00.760
a digital partnership,
they understand

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:02.980
all the responsibilities
and opportunities

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:04.240
that go along with that.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:07.720
And finally, regardless
of how you are using APIs,

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.675
you have to think
about the ecosystems.

00:33:09.675 --> 00:33:11.050
The nature of the
ecosystems will

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:13.120
be different for each
one of those APIs.

00:33:13.120 --> 00:33:15.550
But ultimately,
the moment you've

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:19.810
got one other person beyond the
person who built their API--

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:21.760
built that API using
the API, you now

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:25.780
have an ecosystem that you need
to cultivate and grow and make

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:27.930
successful.

